{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQVLj8VSsoRGyDUjZUjD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Settled:**\n- Discounts are a percentage of the whole order.\n- The discount comes off before VAT, so VAT is 20% of (subtotal − discount).\n- Amounts stay in integer cents. An order without a discount behaves exactly as it does today.\n\n**Choices I'll make unless you object:**\n- The field is `order.discountPercent`, a number from 0 to 100. Decimals like 12.5 are allowed. It defaults to 0, and anything outside that range throws an error.\n- The discount amount is `round(subtotal × percent / 100)`, worked out once per order. That uses the same `Math.round` rule as `tax.js`.\n- `tax()` charges VAT on (subtotal − discount).\n- The invoice shows `discountPercent` and `discountCents`, and its total becomes `subtotal − discount + tax`. The reconcile test is extended to cover this.\n- I'll add tests for: no discount (same results as today), 10%, a percentage that needs rounding, 0% and 100%, and out-of-range values.\n\n**Question:**\n\n3. **How much does a customer get back when they refund a line from a discounted order?** Right now `refundLine` returns the line's net price plus 20% VAT, rounded per line.\n   - **(a) The same percentage off the refunded line, then VAT:** `discounted = round(net × (1 − p/100))`, refund = `discounted + round(discounted × 0.2)`. The customer gets back what they actually paid for that line. Because each line is rounded separately, refunding every line can differ from the order total by a cent or two. Refunds already have this rounding drift today because of VAT.\n   - (b) Same as (a), but the last refund is adjusted so the refunds add up exactly to what was charged. This means the order has to remember what's already been refunded, and today nothing records that.\n   - (c) No change: refunds stay at the full undiscounted price plus VAT. The customer would get back more than they paid.\n\n   I recommend **(a)**. It's correct to the cent per line, needs no new stored state, and keeps the rounding behaviour you already have.\n\nOnce you answer this, I'll implement it and verify it at the Strict level. Because this is money logic, the change will need your review before it's merged."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1311,"cache_read_input_tokens":39387,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1311},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8cabaadf-a16d-4ea4-aa42-34424e0efab3","uuid":"bbe3efef-1449-4bdb-8bfa-dc279649af8e","timestamp":"2026-11-25T23:09:54.338Z","request_id":"req_011ChQVLizYzraCY4gaCwJbo"}
